WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:03.320
- I am going to start with
our first question from Zoom,

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:05.670
which is around journals.

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:07.920
So I love this question
around scientific literature.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.740
I'm open access advocate
for a decade or more.

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:13.830
So the question is, do you
think open source journals

00:00:13.830 --> 00:00:15.660
should be more accessible to the public?

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:17.510
Why or why not?

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:20.703
And so I think maybe there are
two certain questions here.

00:00:20.703 --> 00:00:22.510
There's this open source stuff like

00:00:22.510 --> 00:00:24.660
bioRxiv, medRvix.

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.030
And the og arXiv, which is like the math

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.203
and physics source, pre print server.

00:00:30.416 --> 00:00:33.950
And that's been around for a
really long time by the way.

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:36.500
So math and physics people
have had it going on

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.740
way longer than biologists.

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:39.923
So props to them.

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.940
But the peer reviewed stuff
is really inaccessible.

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:47.460
So I would love to hear
everybody here on our panel,

00:00:47.460 --> 00:00:48.640
like where are you with that?

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.240
Do you think that this
should be open to the public?

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:58.300
- Hmm, so open to the public
as in, like accessible to read.

00:00:58.467 --> 00:00:59.683
- (mumbles) I guess.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:01.943
- I think that's the main thing.

00:01:01.943 --> 00:01:03.680
Atleast that's how I understood it.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.250
And I'm like Jeannie, a very
big advocate of open source

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:09.730
and I have been for a very long time.

00:01:09.730 --> 00:01:14.080
But, I'm interested to see
what Emily and Lindsey think.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.660
- Okay, for that, absolutely yes.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:18.740
I wanted to clarify whether it was like,

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.330
accessible without a pay wall

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:22.040
or accessible in the
way that it's written.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.460
Because I mean, I think
to like to some degree

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.540
that could also be made a bit better.

00:01:26.540 --> 00:01:28.040
In terms of like the clarity with which

00:01:28.040 --> 00:01:29.858
we write about research.

00:01:29.858 --> 00:01:33.540
But definitely having the
research at least free,

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:37.460
like no price, free
accessible to the public,

00:01:37.460 --> 00:01:39.710
should absolutely be
the case, in my opinion.

00:01:40.770 --> 00:01:41.603
Yeah.

00:01:41.603 --> 00:01:44.578
- Yeah I agree completely
with everything Em just said.

00:01:44.578 --> 00:01:46.828
(laughing)

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:49.060
- That's great, yeah I mean I think

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:50.550
it makes complete sense.

00:01:50.550 --> 00:01:52.510
A lot of this research is publicly funded

00:01:52.510 --> 00:01:54.150
so it makes complete sense for the public

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:56.606
to actually have access to the results.

00:01:56.606 --> 00:01:59.720
You know, the peer reviewed
results of this science.

00:01:59.720 --> 00:02:04.090
Not just the archives but
actually open source publications

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.973
like PLOS and others would be, yeah.

00:02:10.115 --> 00:02:11.196
It would be great.

00:02:11.196 --> 00:02:12.029
- There is a couple of open
source journals like that,

00:02:12.029 --> 00:02:12.862
elife, PLOS.

00:02:12.862 --> 00:02:13.900
