00:03:45.673 --> 00:03:47.950
that is a really important
follow up question.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.810
Because obviously we need to
find drugs that can be used

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:54.650
to treat patients that
already have SARS-CoV-2.

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.613
And prophylactic is great, too.

00:03:58.740 --> 00:04:01.550
- Great, so we have
this wonderful question

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.107
from Facebook from Aniesa.

00:04:04.950 --> 00:04:07.360
You didn't not talk about this explicitly,

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:09.590
but if you caught it in the methods,

00:04:09.590 --> 00:04:12.790
how do they know if they
prevented or reduced infections

00:04:12.790 --> 00:04:15.190
with these drugs in the
cell culture experiment?

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:20.200
- Basically the way they did it was,

00:04:20.200 --> 00:04:22.190
I think this might've been
part of the idea behind

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.540
designing it, where they gave
the drug before the virus.

00:04:25.540 --> 00:04:29.170
Is they basically gave different
cells like plus minus drug,

00:04:29.170 --> 00:04:31.890
of the same quantity
of virus and saw like,

00:04:31.890 --> 00:04:33.880
in theory if the drug does nothing,

00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:35.860
you should see the same
degree of infection

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.120
in both conditions.

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:39.140
But if the drug is preventing infection,

00:04:39.140 --> 00:04:41.070
then you would see less infection

00:04:41.070 --> 00:04:43.710
in the cells that received the drug.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:44.850
If that answers the question.

00:04:44.850 --> 00:04:48.263
But that is kinda how
the sought to measure.

00:04:48.263 --> 00:04:49.460
(mumbles) infectivity.

00:04:49.460 --> 00:04:51.540
- How do you look at infectivity?

00:04:51.540 --> 00:04:53.810
